


   =====( CDrom door V1.00 )==========================================


        Trion 'CD rom' Door.

        (c) Copyright Paul Spijkerman, '95-'97. All rights reserved.

        Written for use with the Trion BBS package.


   ===================================================================


   This door is a system to access CDroms and other removable media
   from the Trion BBS package.

   The door is buffered (uses a 16 Kb buffer) to speed up reading
   description files from the CDrom's. This is done to compensate
   for slow acces time and low transfer speeds from the CDrom drive.

   The door doesn't produce requesters when there isn't a CDrom
   in a drive. So it doesn't hang your BBS when you forget to
   place a CD in any drive.

   It will read index files from Amiga and PC cdrom file without
   troubles because it ignores the carriage returns from PC's.

   The door filters ANSI codes from the description/Index files,
   because some CDrom's use ANSI in the index files you
   get ANSI codes in filenames while marking.


   Installation:
   -------------

   Add something like this to you CDrom or file menu.


Cmd "C" , 0-255
   DosCommandSync "execute doors:TrionCDDoor/mount.script"
   CliDoor        "doors:TrionCDDoor/CDDoor ~50 "
EndCmd


   Note: You don't have to add the DosCommandSync "" line, I use
         to mount the CDrom device in my machine so it isn't
         running (making noise) and using memory when it isn't
         used.



    The door needs a 'doors:TrionCDDoor/device.list' file with a list
    of the device names it should use.

    This file is just a list of all the names of the drives you
    want to access from this door.
    Something like:

CD0:
CD1:
CD2:
CD3:

   If the door is started it will show a list of the drives
   including the status of every drive (The disk name, or
   a message that there is no disk present) and after that
   it will show a short description of the disk from the
   configuration file.

   The messages "No disk present." and "Can't find drive." are in red.


   The idea was to make a door which can handle multiple CD players
   by itself.
   It does this by checking all drives in the 'device.list' and
   check if they exist and store the name of CD in memory.
   All the CD's are checked by this version.
   I don't know how fast or slow this is with a CD-changer.

   The idea was to store which CD was in which drive .. so if
   you enter it it shows the stored list ... and Updates it
   if you want to see a CD.
   Problem is that if you change a CD .. you have to look at it
   with the door before the door knows which CD is in it.
   This last bit is shelved until I know how this door works on
   your system (hence the experimental)

   With this door you don't have to change a config when you
   change a CD .. or exchange CD's with drives.



   Configuration files:
   --------------------

   For every CDrom title you should have a a configuration file.
   The name of the file is very important, it shoould be
   exactly the same as the name of the disk with ".cfg"
   added at the end. This is important because the door
   looks at the name of a CD to check which configuration
   file it should load.

   So if you want to use a CD called AMINET10 , it tries to
   load 'AMINET10.cfg'.


   The configuration file uses the format of a configuration file
   someone handed to me because I had no idea how something liked that
   looked like.
   It was the configuration of a Max's BBS CDrom door .. but I have
   now idea if it is used bij others.

   The format is very simple
   For instance:


cd0:
AmiNet BBS (Amiga)
Dunno Yet
Soon
 2 
"Business Proggies - Database1"   "CDX:Files/index"   "CDX:Files/"
"Business Proggies - Database2"   "CDX:Files/index"   "CDX:Files/"


   The first line is the name of the CDrom device.   (Not used in the door anymore)
   The second line is the name or title of the disk. 
   The third line is the name of the CDrom that will be online next.
   The fourth line is the date the next CDrom will be online.
   The fifth line is the number of sections on the CDrom.
   All the folowing lines are information about the sections on this disk.

   The information of the sections is in the folowing format :

"Name of the section"   "Path of the index file"   "Path of the actual files"


   The index files basically consist of the names of the files folowed
   with spaces and a description on one line per file.


   Note that all drive names in the config should be "CDX:", because
   the door doesn't know which drive the medium is in.
   Also with this way you could have index files on harddisk
   if the CDrom has no index files.

   ( "CDX:" in capitals, note that the X is a character not a digit !!!).

   The door wil replace 'CDX:' with the drive name the disk
   was found in ... except of cause if you specify 'DH1:' instead
   of 'CDX:' it will search the index or files on 'DH1:' instead
   of replacing the name.












